/* Wealth Lab Academic · M4 quantitative investing / source-verified · 2026-11-01 */
window.WealthLabAcademicM4Data={"schema":"wealth-lab-m4-academic-v1","date":"2026-11-01","lessonIds":["L4.S1","L4.S2","L4.S3"],"lessons":[{"id":"L4.S1","title":"Stocks, Funds and Ownership","subtitle":"Capital markets, valuation, ownership claims and evidence-based security research","prerequisites":["L1.S1 balance sheet","L1.S3 present value","L4 original gold textbook"],"objectives":["Map primary versus secondary markets and equity claims","Reconcile total return including distributions and frictions","Compare fund structures, NAV, spreads, turnover and fees","Derive simple stock and bond valuation models with sensitivity","Evaluate financial statements, market capitalization and enterprise value","Use credible investor disclosures to build an investment memo"],"sections":[{"title":"Capital markets: who receives money and who owns the claim","body":["A stock represents a class of ownership claim on a corporation; a bond generally represents a contractual debt claim. Buying newly issued shares in a primary offering supplies capital to the issuing company after underwriting and other transaction costs. Buying existing listed shares through a secondary-market transaction ordinarily exchanges cash with another investor, not with the issuing firm. Secondary markets still matter to businesses because liquid trading can lower investors' demanded illiquidity premium, but the distinction prevents a common misconception that every stock trade finances a company's new factory.","Equity holders normally have residual economic exposure after debts and senior claims, along with specific voting, dividend and liquidation rights under their class of security and law. The board can reinvest earnings rather than distribute dividends; a share price can move with revised expected cash flows, discount rates, risk, dilution and investor demand. An investor does not own a direct piece of each corporate asset free of corporate creditors, and a profitable business does not promise a positive stock return at the investor's purchase price.","Trace a hypothetical issuance: a company sells 1 million new shares at $20, before $500,000 expenses. Gross proceeds are $20 million and net proceeds are $19.5 million; existing ownership percentages may be diluted depending on total shares and who subscribes. A secondary-market buyer later purchasing 100 shares at $22 sends roughly $2,200 plus costs to the selling shareholder. That investor's returns depend on future prices and dividends, not on the company's original offering cash."],"worked":["1,000,000 newly issued shares × $20 = $20,000,000 gross equity proceeds.","Less hypothetical $500,000 issuance fees = $19,500,000 company net proceeds.","Secondary trade 100 shares at $22 = $2,200 paid to seller before brokerage costs.","If a company's 10 million shares are outstanding and an investor holds 100, ownership is 0.001% before class differences.","Ownership percentage may be diluted by new share issuance; shareholder claims remain subordinate to creditors."],"practice":[["Explain why secondary market trading can be economically useful without sending its trade proceeds to the corporation.","Secondary trading offers liquidity and price discovery; buyers exchange money with previous investors. Liquidity can indirectly support capital formation, but it is not the same as primary issuance."],["Why can a profitable company's stock lose value after positive earnings are announced?","Investors trade the present value of *expected future* cash flows and risk. Good reported results may still be below expectations, priced in, or offset by higher discount rates."]]},{"title":"Security returns: price change, cash income, reinvestment and timing","body":["Total return combines price appreciation or decline with distributions and financing or trading costs over a defined holding interval. The basic one-period stock return is (ending price−starting price+cash dividends per share)/starting price, assuming a known ex-dividend and payment treatment and excluding taxes and fees. Price return omits cash distributions; total return is the better economic measure when comparing securities that pay different dividends. Realized after-tax returns require transaction dates, costs, tax lots and applicable law.","Arithmetic average returns and geometric compounded returns answer different questions. The arithmetic mean estimates a one-period average under suitable assumptions, while compounded investor wealth follows the product of one plus each period's realized return. A portfolio that earns +20% in year one and −20% in year two ends at 1.2×0.8=0.96 of its starting value, a 4% cumulative loss even though the arithmetic mean is zero. Losses and gains are asymmetric: after a 40% drawdown, returning to the original value requires a 66.67% gain.","Timing of contributions complicates performance evaluation. Time-weighted return separates a manager's investment performance from external contributions by chaining subperiod returns. Money-weighted return, commonly approximated by an internal rate of return on dated investor cash flows, reflects timing and magnitude of contributions. A household investing $250 monthly may realize a different experience from an index's published calendar-year return. Disclose whether dividends are reinvested and whether fees, taxes and inflation are included."],"worked":["Buy 50 shares for $100 each, sell at $108 and receive $3 per share in dividends.","Gross initial cost $5,000, sale proceeds $5,400, dividends $150.","Gross gain $550 and one-period nominal total return $550/$5,000 = 11%.","With $20 total buy/sell fees, net gain $530, 10.6% on a simplified $5,000 opening capital convention.","Two years +20% and −20% turn $1,000 into $960, not $1,000."],"practice":[["Distinguish an 11% price-and-income total return from the 8% price-only return in the stock example.","Price return is ($108−$100)/$100=8%; add $3 of dividends per $100 initial share price to obtain an 11% gross total return before fees and taxes."],["How can the same fund report positive time-weighted performance while a late-contributing investor loses money?","The manager's chained performance removes contribution effects, while the investor's actual purchase prices and cash-flow timing can produce a negative money-weighted experience."]]},{"title":"Market mechanics: bids, asks, orders, settlement and investor protection","body":["A quoted stock price is not a guaranteed transaction price. A market displays bids offered by potential buyers and asks requested by potential sellers; the spread is a trading cost paid indirectly when crossing the market. A market order seeks execution but not an exact price; a limit order sets a maximum buy or minimum sell price but may not execute. Stop orders can become market orders at a trigger, potentially filling far from the stop during volatile trading. The SEC's August 2026 updated investor bulletins are useful primary reading for these distinctions.","Liquidity includes depth at various price levels, the time required to execute, and the market impact of a trade—not merely whether a ticker trades. A thinly traded fund may show a narrow quoted spread for a small order but much worse execution for a large order. The total cost of a transaction can include spread, commissions, price impact, taxes and opportunity cost if a limit order remains unfilled. A professional investment memo describes both expected returns and the mechanics of implementing the allocation.","Customer protection requires a separate distinction. SIPC protection at a member brokerage, subject to law and eligibility, is designed for situations where a brokerage fails and customer securities or cash are missing; standard coverage limits are $500,000 including a $250,000 limit for cash held to buy securities. It does not guarantee investment value or insure market losses. FDIC deposit insurance on eligible bank deposits is a different program. A market decline is not brokerage insolvency."],"worked":["Illustrative bid $49.90, ask $50.10: spread $0.20 a share.","Buy 100 at ask for $5,010 and immediately sell 100 at bid for $4,990, a $20 loss absent price change or commissions.","Cost as share of buy cost = $20/$5,010 ≈0.399%.","A $50 buy limit may not execute when the lowest available seller asks $50.10.","SIPC protections are conditioned on member brokerage failure and missing securities; normal 30% market losses remain possible."],"practice":[["Why can a market order be risky immediately after unexpected company news even if the displayed last trade seems attractive?","Market orders prioritize getting filled, not a promised price. Quotes can move abruptly and thin order-book depth can produce slippage relative to a stale last-traded price."],["Does SIPC reimburse a fund investor if the fund falls by 40% during a recession?","No. SIPC is a limited protection for missing customer assets in eligible broker failures; it does not cover a security's ordinary market decline."]]},{"title":"Funds and ETFs: what shareholders own, NAV and fee drag","body":["An open-end mutual fund issues and redeems shares at net asset value under its pricing procedures, while many ETFs trade intraday on exchanges at market prices that can deviate from underlying NAV. SEC-registered mutual funds and ETFs pool investor capital and hold securities on shareholders' behalf; the investor owns fund shares rather than direct title to every underlying portfolio holding. Some exchange-traded products, such as particular notes and commodity arrangements, are not equivalent to registered investment-company ETFs and require different risk analysis.","Fund expense ratios reduce the economic return experienced by investors over time. A 0.05% expense ratio and a 0.80% expense ratio differ by 0.75 percentage points annually under a simplified same-gross-return comparison. An investor cannot assume that higher fees predict higher net performance. The SEC's July 2025 bulletin explains ongoing fund charges, shareholder fees, trading costs, bid-ask spreads and ETF premiums/discounts. Portfolio turnover and tax distributions may also matter in taxable accounts.","Diversification must be measured by actual exposure, not number of ticker symbols. Two funds can share many of the same large stocks and be correlated; a sector ETF might own dozens of companies yet concentrate in one industry and common economic risk. Compare holdings, index methodology, expense ratio, spread, tracking difference, assets and closure/liquidation risks. A low-cost broad fund may suit a long-horizon core allocation, but no stock fund is an appropriate substitute for cash needed to close a house in a few months."],"worked":["Hypothetical $20,000 investment, annual gross return 6% before fund operating charges.","Approximate net return: 5.95% at 0.05% fee versus 5.20% at 0.80% fee; actual fee timing differs.","At 20 years, illustrated terminal balance $20,000×1.0595^20 versus $20,000×1.052^20.","The cost gap compounds as the lower fee leaves more capital invested.","A narrow sector fund is not diversified across the entire stock market merely because it holds 50 stocks."],"practice":[["Why is ETF share price not guaranteed to equal its published net asset value in every trade?","ETF shares are traded with bids and asks, and the market price can carry a premium or discount to NAV. Underlying-market liquidity and fund structure affect the magnitude."],["Can holding two funds be less diversified than holding one?","Yes. Two funds with heavily overlapping holdings or the same sector exposures may not reduce the portfolio's correlated risk, while one broad fund can already hold many asset classes or issuers."]]},{"title":"Reading corporate financial statements without confusing profit and cash","body":["Company analysis begins with the income statement, balance sheet and cash-flow statement. Revenue measures recognized sales, but not collected cash; net income reflects accounting recognition and expense estimates. Operating cash flow reconciles net income to cash movements including noncash charges and working capital. Capital expenditures may be needed to sustain assets even when reported profits look attractive. Free cash flow has several definitions; a common starting measure is operating cash flow minus capital expenditures.","Debt and equity claims make enterprise and per-share valuation differ. Equity market capitalization is share price multiplied by current basic or appropriately diluted shares outstanding. Enterprise value for a straightforward company is often approximated as equity capitalization plus interest-bearing debt minus excess cash, with adjustments for preferred securities, minority interests, leases and operating cash needs. Buying a company with $1 billion market cap and $500 million debt is not the same economic exposure as buying a debt-free $1 billion enterprise.","Analysts evaluate profitability, cash conversion, leverage, reinvestment and share count trends over multiple periods. Buybacks can reduce shares outstanding but are not necessarily value-creating if done at an excessive price or financed by risky debt. Revenue growth can coexist with shrinking margins or negative free cash flow. SEC filings, including Forms 10-K and 10-Q, management discussion, risk factors and audit opinions, are more suitable source documents than an unverified social-media earnings claim."],"worked":["Company net income $100m; operating cash flow $120m; capital expenditures $70m.","Simplified free cash flow = $120m−$70m=$50m, not $100m net income.","100m shares at $20 ⇒ equity capitalization $2.0bn.","Debt $400m, excess cash $150m ⇒ simplified EV ≈$2.25bn.","FCF yield relative to equity capitalization $50m/$2.0bn=2.5%, but valuation depends on cash obligations and growth."],"practice":[["Why can a company report positive earnings and still struggle to pay creditors?","Accrual earnings can include noncash items and revenue not yet collected, while debt service and capital expenditure require available cash. Examine cash flow, working capital and maturities."],["What additional facts should be checked before labeling a company cheap because its P/E ratio is below peers?","Growth, profitability quality, debt, cyclicality, dilution, one-off earnings, risk and business differences can explain a lower multiple; a simple ratio is not a valuation conclusion."]]},{"title":"Discounted cash flow valuation as a model, not a magic fair price","body":["Valuation starts with a conditional principle: the value of a financial claim reflects the present value of cash flows its owner expects to receive, adjusted for timing and risk. In a constant-growth dividend discount model, P0=D1/(k−g) when k is greater than g and the assumptions are economically sustainable, where D1 is next-period dividend, k the required return and g constant growth. A company without stable distributions may be better analyzed through free cash flow to equity or enterprise, with explicit capital requirements and terminal assumptions.","The model is sensitive. If next year's dividend is $2, the required return is 9% and perpetual growth 3%, simplified value is $2/(0.09−0.03)=$33.33. If k rises to 10% holding g at 3%, value becomes $28.57. The nearly 14.3% decline reflects only a one-point discount-rate shift. If k approaches g, the mathematical value explodes; this warns of unsuitable assumptions, not infinite real investment worth.","Professional analysis uses pessimistic, base and optimistic cases with a clear research trail. Forecast a finite period of revenues, operating margins, taxes, reinvestment, and free cash flows; then disclose a terminal value assumption. Link a price target to its required return, capital structure and dilution. A range of values is more responsible than a precise price to two decimals when forecast uncertainty is large. Never claim that a DCF model guarantees a future market price."],"worked":["Next-year dividend D1=$2 per share, required return k=9%, perpetual growth g=3%.","Dividend discount value P0=$2/(0.09−0.03)=$33.33 per share.","If required return rises to 10%, P0=$2/(0.10−0.03)=$28.57.","Changing g to 4% at k=9% gives $40; show why growth assumptions matter.","The model is invalid if k≤g for an indefinite constant-growth perpetuity."],"practice":[["Why is assuming 8% perpetual dividend growth and an 8% required return mathematically invalid under the simple Gordon model?","The denominator k−g is zero, so the perpetuity does not have a finite model value. Economic sustainability and finite-horizon structure must be reassessed."],["Would a model value of $33.33 guarantee that the stock trades there next year?","No. It is conditional on uncertain dividends, discount rates, growth and timing. Market price is determined by actual trades and changing expectations."]]},{"title":"Fixed-income claims and the inverse relation between price and yield","body":["A bond's price is the discounted value of promised coupon and principal payments, adjusted for credit and contractual options. For a plain fixed-coupon bond with N annual payments, coupon C, face F and required yield y, price is P=Σ[C/(1+y)^t]+F/(1+y)^N. When required market yield rises, the same fixed future payments are discounted more heavily and price generally falls. A bond held to maturity may still expose the investor to default, reinvestment and liquidity risks; bond funds typically have fluctuating net asset values and no promise to return a given investor's purchase price.","Duration approximates sensitivity of bond price to a small yield change: ΔP/P≈−modified duration×Δy, with convexity and other corrections for larger moves. A fund displaying a modified duration of 6 years may experience roughly a 6% price decline for a parallel one-percentage-point rate increase under a local approximation. Duration is a sensitivity measure, not a guarantee of return, holding period or maximum loss. Credit spreads and option features can change the outcome.","For a zero-coupon bond, P=F/(1+y)^N. A $1,000 face bond due in three years at 4% annual yield is worth about $889.00. If the yield rises to 5%, price falls to about $863.84. This can be worked precisely without forecasts. Compare individual Treasuries, corporate bonds, bond funds, certificates of deposit and cash deposits on credit protection, price risk, maturity, after-tax return, early-sale consequences and whether funds are required soon."],"worked":["Zero-coupon bond: face $1,000, maturity 3 years, yield 4% ⇒ $1,000/1.04³≈$889.00.","At yield 5%, price $1,000/1.05³≈$863.84, a market value loss of about $25.16.","Illustrative bond fund modified duration 6 and yield rise 1 percentage point ⇒ approximately 6% price decline, before convexity and other effects.","A fixed coupon does not prevent resale value from changing when yields or credit risk change.","Bond fund shares generally do not have the same fixed individual-bond maturity cash payment."],"practice":[["Why can an investment-grade bond fund decline when interest rates rise even if its holdings continue paying interest?","Market discount rates change the present value of fixed future coupons and principal. Longer-duration holdings are especially sensitive, even without defaults."],["Why should a home buyer avoid equating a three-year bond fund's price exposure with a bank deposit guaranteed for a known withdrawal date?","The fund has fluctuating NAV and market risk; eligible insured bank deposits follow different contractual and insurance protections, subject to account terms."]]},{"title":"Investor research: investment thesis, counterevidence and implementation","body":["An investment thesis is a falsifiable explanation of what must happen for the security to justify its current price. Start with business economics, asset and liability structure, expected cash flows, valuation range, key risks, position size and the household goal. Identify a disconfirming condition, such as falling free cash flow, unexpected refinancing at higher rates, dilution, deteriorating margins or weak governance. Price momentum or enthusiasm on social media is not sufficient evidence of a margin of safety.","Research process matters as much as formulas. The investor should trace security names and share classes to EDGAR filings or a fund prospectus, read fee tables, inspect holdings and concentration, identify liquidity and order implementation, and verify tax lots and account treatment. Record assumptions and source dates. An unreviewed fund marketed as 'diversified' might be a levered, inverse or highly concentrated product not appropriate for a long-term household portfolio.","Finish with a decision table: buy under defined valuation and liquidity conditions, watch for better evidence or price, or reject because the investment is inappropriate for the goal. A family saving for a down payment in twelve months may rationally reject an attractive-looking stock because a market drawdown could prevent closing. For money with a 20-year horizon and a sufficient emergency reserve, the acceptable risk set can differ. The same ticker can be inappropriate for one goal and reasonable for another.","Document that research is not a guaranteed forecast or personalized securities advice. Require a student investment memorandum to show the price paid, costs, expected cash flows, scenario valuation, one test of counterevidence, worst credible loss and effect on the household's obligations. That standard makes Module 4 a training in disciplined decision-making instead of collecting investment terminology."],"worked":["Household has $8,000 earmarked for a home closing in twelve months and $5,000 for 20-year investing.","An illustrative 30% stock decline would erase $2,400 of the closing bucket if wrongly invested there.","Keeping the closing funds in an appropriately liquid vehicle preserves the transaction goal better under this scenario.","A $5,000 long-horizon investment can absorb different volatility if the household has adequate reserves and affordability.","Investment memo must separate expected upside, source dates, downside, account fees and when cash will be needed."],"practice":[["Why could declining an apparently undervalued stock be rational when a home closing is near?","The household's required cash date and inability to absorb a drawdown may dominate expected investment return; opportunity cost should be assessed against the specific goal."],["Name two pieces of primary documentation a student must inspect before recommending a publicly traded stock or fund.","For stock: relevant Form 10-K/10-Q and disclosures. For a fund: its prospectus including fee table and holdings or shareholder reports; records should show dates and security identification."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["SEC Investor.gov · Stocks","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/stocks"],["SEC Investor.gov · Mutual Fund and ETF Characteristics (2025)","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/general-resources/news-alerts/alerts-bulletins/characteristics-mutual-funds-exchange-traded-funds"],["SEC Investor.gov · Mutual Fund and ETF Fees (2025)","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/general-resources/news-alerts/alerts-bulletins/investor-bulletins/mutual-fund-and-etf-fees-and-expenses-investor-bulletin"],["SEC Investor.gov · Understanding Order Types (updated Aug 2026)","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/general-resources/news-alerts/alerts-bulletins/investor-bulletins-14"],["SEC Investor.gov · Bond fund risks","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/glossary/bond-funds-and-income-funds"],["SIPC · What SIPC Protects","https://www.sipc.org/for-investors/introduction"],["SEC EDGAR filings","https://www.sec.gov/edgar/search/"]],"assessment":{"title":"Security analysis committee memo","prompt":"Compare one hypothetical $5,000 equity purchase and one fixed-income alternative. Reconcile total return, execution costs, fund expense drag, company cash flow, dividend-discount sensitivity and yield shock on a bond. Include at least one falsifying investment-thesis condition, a short-horizon home down-payment constraint, and six primary-source references. State assumptions rather than assert investment superiority.","rubric":["Market claims and legal product differences (15%)","Total-return and transaction math (20%)","Fund and company fundamental analysis (20%)","DCF or bond pricing sensitivity (20%)","Appropriateness, counterevidence, sources and presentation (25%)"]}},{"id":"L4.S2","title":"Risk, Time and Liquidity","subtitle":"Quantitative portfolio risk, diversification, cash-flow horizons and robust decision-making","prerequisites":["L1.S2 cash-flow timing","L1.S3 compounding and inflation","L4.S1 security returns and claims"],"objectives":["Calculate expected return and weighted portfolio exposure","Derive two-asset variance and interpret correlation","Measure standard deviation, drawdown, volatility drag and loss recovery","Stress-test goal-based savings under negative market returns","Implement a rebalance rule that respects costs, taxes and cash constraints","Create an investment policy with credible downside and liquidity buffers"],"sections":[{"title":"Risk is multiple dimensions, not one volatility number","body":["An investor faces market risk, credit risk, inflation risk, liquidity risk, concentration risk, counterparty risk, leverage risk, regulatory risk and the risk of failing to fund a goal on time. These may be correlated during crises. Volatility measured from historical returns describes one aspect of uncertainty, not every risk. A deposit may have low nominal price volatility but still lose purchasing power; a long-duration government bond may have low default probability but significant interim price risk; a broad stock fund can be diversified across firms while exposed to economy-wide losses.","Risk tolerance is willingness to experience uncertainty, whereas risk capacity reflects whether a household can financially survive a loss without breaching necessary obligations. Time horizon states when the money is needed, not how long the buyer likes a particular fund. A $20,000 home-closing reserve due in one year has a different loss capacity from $20,000 of retirement money expected to remain invested for decades. Failure to distinguish these questions can make high expected returns irrelevant.","Document four risk categories for every allocation: the chance of a loss, the potential size of a loss, the ability to recover before a spending deadline, and the possibility of illiquid exit. A risk register can list the source of exposure and controls such as diversification, appropriately liquid reserves and debt limits. Hedging can itself cost money and fail to remove all risk. Never state that a stock's past performance or a fund's legal registration ensures its future return."],"worked":["Household liquid account $16,000, of which $10,000 is due for closing in twelve months.","A 25% decline on $10,000 invested in equities could leave $7,500, a $2,500 closing shortfall.","The same $2,500 drawdown may be tolerable in a distant retirement goal if reserves and expenses are protected.","A 3% inflation year reduces the purchasing power of an unchanged $10,000 nominal balance to about $9,708.74 in prior-year dollars.","Categorize nominal loss risk, real-value loss risk, timing risk and liquidity risk separately."],"practice":[["Why is a cash account not 'risk free' merely because its dollar balance does not fluctuate?","Inflation may erode its real purchasing power, institutions and product terms matter, and opportunities can be forgone. Low market-price volatility is not zero economic risk."],["What makes two households with identical risk tolerance different in appropriate equity allocation?","Their income stability, obligations, time horizon, existing debt, insurance, accessible reserves and ability to delay withdrawals can differ materially."]]},{"title":"Weighted returns and why arithmetic averages cannot predict wealth","body":["For an unlevered portfolio with beginning weights w_i and single-period security returns R_i, portfolio holding-period return is R_p=Σw_iR_i, provided weights sum to one and cash flows, fees and rebalancing timing are handled consistently. The formula is exact for a period whose weights are established at the beginning and whose constituents' returns are measured on the same basis. Expected portfolio return is E[R_p]=Σw_iE[R_i], but each constituent expectation is an uncertain modeling input.","Assume a portfolio begins with 60% stocks and 40% bonds. If stocks earn +12% over a year and bonds −3%, the no-external-cash-flow period return is 0.60×12%+0.40×(−3%)=6%. The portfolio worth $10,000 becomes $10,600 before costs and taxes. Its ending equity share rises because stocks outperformed, so future weights drift. A rebalancing policy must specify whether the investor trades at period end or lets this new exposure persist.","Arithmetic and geometric means matter particularly when money compounds. A two-year sequence of +30% then −20% gives arithmetic mean +5%, but cumulative value factor 1.3×0.8=1.04, equivalent to about +1.98% compound annual growth. The order of those two annual returns does not change ending wealth without cash flows, but adding or withdrawing funds at different times makes return sequence economically decisive."],"worked":["Starting portfolio $10,000, 60% equity and 40% bond ⇒ $6,000 equity, $4,000 bonds.","Stocks +12% ⇒ $6,720; bonds −3% ⇒ $3,880; total $10,600.","One-year return $600/$10,000=6%, ending equity weight $6,720/$10,600≈63.40%.","+30% then −20% compounded factor 1.04, not arithmetic mean +5% per year.","Two-year geometric mean (1.04)^(1/2)−1≈1.98% annually before costs."],"practice":[["Calculate the one-year return of a 70/30 portfolio if equities lose 15% and bonds gain 4%.","0.70×(−15%)+0.30×4%=−9.3%. A $10,000 initial account becomes $9,070 before fees and cash flows."],["Why must weights be defined at the beginning of the measurement period to calculate realized weighted return?","Asset weights change as prices move; combining ending weights with full-period security returns can double-count drift and give the wrong portfolio result."]]},{"title":"Variance, covariance and why correlation drives diversification","body":["Two assets may each be volatile while their combined portfolio is less volatile if they do not move in lockstep. Portfolio variance for two assets is σp²=w₁²σ₁²+w₂²σ₂²+2w₁w₂σ₁σ₂ρ, where ρ is their return correlation and σ denotes standard deviation measured over the same interval. The covariance contribution is the cross term. The weights, volatilities and correlation must be aligned to identical return frequencies and assumptions. Historical estimates are uncertain and may change substantially during a crisis.","Consider a 60% stock asset with 20% assumed annual volatility, a 40% bond asset with 6%, and correlation 0.20. Portfolio variance equals (0.6×0.20)²+(0.4×0.06)²+2×0.6×0.4×0.20×0.06×0.20=0.016128. Portfolio standard deviation is about 12.70%. At correlation 1, standard deviation would be exactly 0.6×20%+0.4×6%=14.4% for positive weights; lower correlation reduces modeled portfolio volatility.","Correlation is not causation, nor does low historical correlation guarantee a hedge in distress. Equity and bond markets sometimes fall simultaneously; inflation and changing rates can drive common losses. Risk management also requires evaluating tail losses and illiquidity. A portfolio's average standard deviation cannot promise the size of its next drawdown. Use assumptions as scenario controls and ask whether a correlation spike invalidates the investment policy."],"worked":["Stock weight 0.60 and volatility 0.20; bond weight 0.40 and volatility 0.06.","Correlation ρ=0.20 ⇒ variance (0.12)²+(0.024)²+2×0.6×0.4×0.20×0.06×0.20=0.016128.","Annual volatility ≈sqrt(0.016128)=12.70%.","Correlation ρ=1 gives 14.40% modeled volatility; ρ=−1 would give |0.12−0.024|=9.60%.","No one of these assumed statistics is a guaranteed portfolio-loss boundary."],"practice":[["What does the covariance term represent in the two-asset portfolio variance formula?","It measures how the assets' return movements interact, with size determined by weights, volatilities and correlation. That interaction can raise or lower overall variance."],["Why is a low historical correlation between assets not evidence that one will reliably protect against an extreme equity crash?","Market relationships can change with regimes and common shocks; estimates contain sampling error and can rise in crises. Stress testing should consider correlation breakdown."]]},{"title":"Drawdowns, sequence risk and the asymmetric road back","body":["Volatility is an average dispersion; drawdown tracks actual wealth decline from a previous high. Maximum drawdown is the largest peak-to-trough percentage loss in the chosen sample period, which depends on the return path. After a 30% fall an account needs 1/0.70−1≈42.86% gain to recover its original nominal level. After a 50% fall it needs 100%. This arithmetic is why aggressive strategies can demand unrealistic subsequent performance after a large loss.","Sequence risk matters most when withdrawals or contributions are occurring. Consider a retiree with $100,000 who withdraws $10,000 at the start of each year. If the portfolio loses 20% in year one and gains 25% in year two, the account evolves from $100,000 to $90,000 after first withdrawal, then $72,000 after loss; second withdrawal lowers it to $62,000 and a 25% gain takes it to $77,500. Reverse the return order and the result is different even though the two-year compounded market return is the same. Cash-flow timing changes the amount exposed to each return.","Home buyers face a similar short-horizon sequence risk: the market can decline immediately before earnest money or closing is due. Investors should hold funding required at known dates in appropriately reliable and liquid forms rather than calculate only a long-run average forecast. Maintaining sufficient reserves and avoiding forced sales can improve the odds of reaching an investment horizon, but does not eliminate the possibility of major losses."],"worked":["Starting $100,000, withdraw $10,000 at each year's beginning.","Loss-first path: ($100,000−$10,000)×0.80=$72,000; then ($72,000−$10,000)×1.25=$77,500.","Gain-first path: ($100,000−$10,000)×1.25=$112,500; then ($112,500−$10,000)×0.80=$82,000.","Both market-return sequences compound to 1.00 in isolation; investor balances differ by $4,500.","Recovery after −40% requires 1/0.6−1=66.67% gain, not merely 40%."],"practice":[["Explain why a retiree with fixed withdrawals can experience very different ending wealth under the same returns in a different order.","Withdrawals remove shares or capital at different valuations, changing how much is invested for the later return. Poor early returns are particularly damaging when cash must be withdrawn."],["What does a 35% drawdown require to restore nominal account value without contributions?","A recovery gain of 1/0.65−1≈53.85%, before fees and inflation. The prior percentage loss and required recovery are not equal."]]},{"title":"Goal-based buckets, liquidity and the mortgage down-payment constraint","body":["Household investing must coordinate with liabilities, not just asset returns. A disciplined plan can define cash needed for bills and emergency reserves, a separate home purchase fund with a dated target, and a long-term retirement pool. The buckets are accounting and behavioral controls, not different laws of economics. Every dollar assigned to a short-term purchase cannot simultaneously be invested for the distant horizon without increasing the risk of an underfunded closing.","Suppose the household has $18,000 cash, owes $2,000 in near-term bills, wants $10,000 minimum emergency reserves, and expects to need $12,000 for a property closing within one year. Available cash after near bills and the desired reserve is $6,000, which leaves a $6,000 purchase gap. An apparently attractive $5,000 speculative trade cannot fix that funding gap reliably: a 30% decline would leave $3,500 and worsen the plan. A sources-and-uses schedule shows that the goal requires more saving, a lower purchase obligation, more time or documented alternative funding.","Emergency cash and a short-term home fund should be assessed for access, credit exposure, insurance eligibility where applicable, maturity and inflation—not simply which account shows the highest current yield. The return on a risky asset is not a substitute for a guaranteed dated obligation. A household able to invest $250 monthly could direct initial contributions toward the cash gap until the purchase plan is secure, then revisit long-term allocations."],"worked":["Cash $18,000 minus near bills $2,000 minus target emergency reserve $10,000 = $6,000 toward home closing.","Closing target $12,000 yields $6,000 funding deficit before fees or unexpected expenses.","At $250 saved per month without interest, gap takes 24 deposits to close; at $500 per month, 12 deposits.","Putting $5,000 into an asset losing 30% yields $3,500, reducing possible closing cash by $1,500.","Annual return assumptions cannot be treated as guaranteed means to meet a specific closing deadline."],"practice":[["How would you explain to a new investor why stock-market expected returns should not finance a fixed home closing date in eight months?","Expected returns reflect uncertain outcomes; a near-term market decline can make the required cash unavailable precisely when due. Liquidity and reliability dominate long-term upside for that obligation."],["What is the monthly contribution needed to close a $6,000 gap in 15 months ignoring interest?","$6,000/15=$400 per month. Verify that amount can be saved after essential expenses rather than assuming it is affordable."]]},{"title":"Rebalancing, concentration and investment policy rules","body":["A 60/40 portfolio rarely remains 60/40 after markets move. Rebalancing can return exposures toward the investor's selected long-run risk budget, but trading can create costs and taxable gains. Policies may rebalance on calendar dates, at weight drift thresholds, or by directing new contributions to underweight assets. No frequency is universally best. A robust plan states target weights, permitted ranges, eligible investment products, cash minimums, and exceptions for tax or liquidity.","Take the prior $10,600 portfolio with $6,720 in equities and $3,880 bonds. At 60/40 target, desired equity is $6,360 and bonds $4,240. Exact rebalancing sells $360 of equity and buys $360 of bonds, ignoring fees and taxes. An alternative is to direct $900 in new deposits entirely to bonds, giving equities $6,720 and bonds $4,780, for total $11,500 and equity weight about 58.43%; new-money-only rebalancing can avoid realizing taxable gains but may miss an exact target.","Concentration includes more than holding a large single stock. Employment income linked to the same industry as a company stock position increases household-wide exposure. Housing ownership in the same regional market can also correlate with job security and local economic shocks. Analyze the family's 'economic balance sheet' including wages, debt and real estate when setting risk limits. A written investment policy converts an emotional reaction to a market decline into a review against predetermined, evidence-based constraints."],"worked":["After hypothetical returns: equity $6,720, bonds $3,880, total $10,600.","Exact 60% equity target $6,360, requiring $360 sale of equity before tax or transaction effects.","Bond target $4,240, requiring $360 purchase.","Alternative $900 new money directed to bonds yields $6,720 equity/$4,780 bonds, total $11,500.","Equity share after contributions ≈58.43%, showing that rebalancing choice affects future exposure."],"practice":[["Why could rebalancing through new contributions be attractive in a taxable brokerage account?","It can reduce the need to sell appreciated investments and realize gains, though it may not restore target weights exactly or quickly enough for the investor's risk constraints."],["Give an example of household concentration that a fund holdings report alone would miss.","An employee whose wages depend on the technology sector and who holds concentrated employer stock may face simultaneous job loss and portfolio decline during a sector downturn."]]},{"title":"Measuring downside scenarios, inflation and fee-adjusted goals","body":["Monte Carlo methods are computational experiments that simulate many possible return sequences under declared probability assumptions. A simulated distribution is not a factual prediction; model choice, return estimation, fat tails, correlation assumptions, inflation, fees and taxes matter. When probability parameters are uncertain, deterministic stress testing can be clearer: calculate the exact effect of a 30% market decline, income interruption, high inflation or an early withdrawal. Never label a single simulated probability of success as guaranteed.","Inflation translates nominal final wealth into purchasing power. If an account reaches $100,000 after ten years and the price index rose 3% every year, real purchasing power in today's dollars would be $100,000/1.03^10≈$74,409. Investment fees compound as a drag. The illustrative difference between 6% gross annual returns with 0.10% fees and 1.00% fees can materially affect decades of compounding. Real returns cannot be approximated perfectly by simply subtracting inflation and expenses over long horizons; use the ratio of growth factors.","Make downside thresholds explicit. A $30,000 portfolio meant to fund a home improvement in three years might face a 25% loss, leaving $22,500. Even if its expected future return is positive, the family may need $30,000 on schedule. A long-horizon portfolio with no imminent withdrawal can potentially withstand larger fluctuations, but only if income, debt and behavior allow the investor to stay invested. Include a cash buffer and a written response to downside conditions instead of relying on a forecast's central line."],"worked":["Nominal value $100,000 after ten years; assumed inflation 3% annually.","Real value $100,000/(1.03)^10≈$74,409 in today's purchasing power.","Portfolio $30,000 with 25% adverse return ⇒ $22,500 and $7,500 funding gap.","To recover from $22,500 to $30,000 requires 33.33% gain, ignoring further inflation and cost.","If annual fee difference is 0.90 percentage points, study compounding impact rather than only first-year dollars."],"practice":[["Why is a Monte Carlo 80% success estimate not proof that a plan will work four times out of five in reality?","It is conditional on chosen probability distributions, return estimates, spending assumptions and model specification; actual future regimes can depart sharply from the simulated world."],["What is the exact real-return factor for 6% nominal growth and 3% inflation over one year?","1.06/1.03≈1.029126, or about 2.91% real return before taxes and other costs, not exactly 3%."]]},{"title":"Write a defensible risk and allocation memorandum","body":["A portfolio decision memo should begin with goals expressed as amounts and dates. The investment policy must list liquidity minimums, contribution affordability, target asset exposures, rebalancing triggers, acceptable instruments, tax status, and risk capacity. It should also identify uncertainties in expected returns rather than infer the future from a decade-long average. The advisor's first obligation is not maximizing modeled final wealth but ensuring the household can fulfill essential obligations under credible shocks.","Construct a base scenario with monthly $250 contributions for twenty years, a conservative return case, a 30% loss case early in accumulation, and a late loss case just before withdrawal. Include realistic fees and an inflation-adjusted objective. A student should calculate contributions separately from investment gains, quantify downside and distinguish nominal terminal value from real purchasing power. This creates a coherent bridge between Module 1's wealth equation and future advanced investment and retirement models.","Finish by explaining what facts would change the recommendation. Examples include unstable employment, inability to maintain emergency cash, high-interest debt, nearer home purchases, retirement plan matching contributions, or a credible need to support a dependent. A plan that depends on using credit card borrowing to avoid selling investments in a crash may be materially riskier than its asset allocation suggests.","An academically complete response must show independent calculations and critique the model. It should state sampling uncertainty, correlation sensitivity, the limits of conventional standard deviation, sources of tail risk and the practical costs of trading. The final rubric rewards well-justified decisions under uncertainty, not confidence in a specific future market outcome."],"worked":["Household contribution $250/month ×240 months = $60,000 principal contributions over 20 years.","At illustrative monthly compounded 6% nominal return, annuity future value ≈$115,510 before fees and inflation.","A 30% drawdown on a balance of $40,000 yields $28,000; required recovery to $40,000 is 42.86%.","A separate emergency reserve should not be included as an equity-return-earning asset in the model.","Report at least three risk scenarios and explicit rebalancing rules."],"practice":[["Why is protecting the ability to remain invested part of a sound long-run equity strategy?","Forced selling after a market decline to pay bills can realize losses and destroy the intended investment horizon; sufficient cash and sustainable obligations support long-run risk capacity."],["What key limitation should accompany a portfolio-risk estimate based on only three years of return data?","Small samples may give unstable volatility, correlation and return estimates and miss unusual market events, so the projections need broader evidence and severe stress scenarios."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["SEC Investor.gov · Asset Allocation and Diversification","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/getting-started/asset-allocation"],["SEC Investor.gov · Beginner's Guide to Diversification","https://www.investor.gov/additional-resources/general-resources/publications-research/info-sheets/beginners-guide-asset"],["SEC Investor.gov · How Fees Affect Your Portfolio, 2025","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/general-resources/news-alerts/alerts-bulletins/investor-bulletins/updated"],["FINRA · Fund Analyzer","https://tools.finra.org/fund_analyzer/"],["SEC Investor.gov · Investor Tips for 2026","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/general-resources/news-alerts/alerts-bulletins/investor-bulletins/investorgov-tips-2026-investor-bulletin"]],"assessment":{"title":"Quantitative investment policy and risk evaluation","prompt":"Create a 60/40 portfolio risk model with stock volatility 20%, bond volatility 6% and correlations −0.5, 0.2 and 1.0. Calculate weighted return under a 12%/−3% scenario, ending weights, exact rebalancing trade and 10-year inflation stress. Compare a $250/month accumulation path under two return sequences with withdrawals. Write an investment policy for a household planning a Connecticut home purchase in 18 months, with an explicit cash-floor constraint and alternative allocations.","rubric":["Portfolio expected and realized returns (20%)","Variance, covariance and correlation sensitivity (25%)","Drawdown and sequence-of-returns analysis (20%)","Liquidity/goals/rebalancing economics (20%)","Risk limitations, citations and professional judgment (15%)"]}},{"id":"L4.S3","title":"Investing with Tax Advantages","subtitle":"Taxable accounts, retirement wrappers, 2026 contribution rules, tax basis and household optimization","prerequisites":["L4.S1 funds and total returns","L4.S2 portfolio risk and withdrawal horizons","L1.S3 time value of money","L2.S2 home cash planning"],"objectives":["Distinguish investment assets from account-level tax treatment","Compare traditional, Roth and taxable after-tax savings with explicit assumptions","Apply 2026 IRS IRA and 401(k) limits and income eligibility conditions","Classify ordinary income, qualified dividends, capital gains, tax lots and wash-sale adjustments","Analyze retirement withdrawal rules against a near-term home purchase","Design an evidence-based savings hierarchy that protects liquidity and avoids tax overclaims"],"sections":[{"title":"The investment and the account are not the same decision","body":["A stock index fund can be held in a taxable brokerage account, a traditional IRA, a Roth IRA or an eligible employer retirement plan. The economic fund owns essentially the same securities, but tax rules differ by wrapper, contribution type and withdrawal event. A taxable brokerage generally does not promise retirement-account deferral or qualified distribution treatment. Traditional retirement contributions may be deductible or made pre-tax if statutory and plan requirements are satisfied, with ordinary income taxation typically applying to later distributions. Roth contributions use after-tax dollars and potentially qualified tax-free withdrawals.","Tax location should follow the financial goal. A household expecting to buy a home within twelve months must ensure accessible funds for closing and reserves before chasing retirement-account tax advantages that can restrict access. Conversely, an individual investing for decades may forgo meaningful employer match or deferral advantages by placing every spare dollar into a taxable account. These are not universal rankings: employer plan terms, fees, income eligibility, tax brackets, debt, emergency reserve and retirement age must be modeled together.","The student should map each dollar from payroll or bank account into its destination and determine when it can be accessed, when taxes may arise and whether contributions count toward a federal limit. A $250 monthly allocation is $3,000 annually, not $250 annually. If $150 goes to Roth retirement and $100 to a taxable home fund every month, annual contributions are $1,800 and $1,200 respectively before earnings. This split is economically meaningful only if bills and reserves are already adequately funded."],"worked":["A hypothetical $250 monthly savings budget equals $3,000 annual contributions.","Allocate $150/month to Roth IRA and $100/month to designated home cash: $1,800 and $1,200 annually.","The stock or bond inside an IRA is still subject to market loss; its tax wrapper does not guarantee return.","An IRA contribution can be limited by compensation, age-based rules and other eligibility requirements.","Liquidity and tax effects must be evaluated separately for each account."],"practice":[["Why is an S&P 500 fund inside a Roth IRA not a different asset class from the same fund held in a taxable account?","Underlying companies and market exposures are essentially the same; the account wrapper changes tax and legal withdrawal treatment, not the investment's fundamental market risk."],["How can a tax-advantaged account be inappropriate for a particular dollar needed soon?","Withdrawal restrictions, taxes or penalties on some distributions and market losses can conflict with a dated expense even if the account offers potential long-run tax savings."]]},{"title":"Traditional versus Roth accounts: put the same tax basis on both sides","body":["A fair comparison requires holding the economic cost of contributing constant. Suppose a worker can sacrifice $1,000 of pre-tax compensation and faces a 20% illustrative marginal tax rate today. A qualified pretax retirement contribution can invest $1,000; a Roth investment using the same gross compensation has $800 available after the illustrative current tax. If both grow by the same factor and traditional withdrawals are taxed at 20%, ending take-home amounts are equal. This algebra explains why tax-rate changes, rules and employer contributions matter more than claiming Roth is automatically superior.","With a fixed gross earning amount P, growth factor G, current marginal rate t_now and future effective tax treatment t_later, simplified traditional spendable value is P×G×(1−t_later), while Roth spendable value is P×(1−t_now)×G assuming a qualified Roth distribution and no other taxes or restrictions. If the future relevant rate is higher than today's, Roth may be favored by the illustration; if lower, traditional may be. Actual marginal tax savings, federal/state taxes, deductions, employer match and nonqualified withdrawals can make the real comparison much more complex.","Traditional IRA deductibility depends on coverage and income, while Roth IRA eligibility depends on income and filing status; some employer plan arrangements allow different contribution types. A traditional IRA contribution is not guaranteed to be deductible simply because the account is named 'traditional.' Some after-tax contributions create basis that must be tracked using tax forms. In an academic analysis, classify contribution tax treatment and tax ownership before calculating terminal wealth.","Do not invent a promised future tax bracket. Use at least three scenarios and say whose tax rate is being modeled. Retirement account design should be coordinated with a legitimate budget, emergency savings and future inheritance wishes. Beneficiaries can face different inherited-retirement distribution rules from the original owner's rules."],"worked":["Pre-tax compensation available for contribution P=$1,000; today's illustrative tax rate 20%.","Traditional contribution $1,000 and Roth contribution $800 from the same gross compensation.","If each grows by factor 2, pretax traditional account $2,000, after 20% withdrawal tax spendable $1,600.","Roth $800 grows to $1,600, assumed fully qualified withdrawal ⇒ $1,600 spendable.","At 30% future tax, traditional spendable falls to $1,400 while qualified Roth remains $1,600 in this simplified comparison."],"practice":[["Why is comparing a $1,000 traditional contribution with a $1,000 Roth contribution not necessarily holding household cost equal?","The Roth $1,000 generally costs after-tax dollars, while a fully deductible traditional $1,000 contribution can reduce current tax. Equal nominal contributions can use different amounts of current disposable resources."],["If the future tax rate is 10% instead of 20% in the illustrative example, what does $2,000 traditional account become after tax?","$2,000×0.90=$1,800, above the modeled qualified Roth $1,600 when current marginal rate was 20%. This is a conditional illustration, not a personal tax projection."]]},{"title":"2026 IRA, 401(k) and income-eligibility rules from the IRS","body":["The IRS publishes annual limits that must be treated as year-specific facts. For 2026, the combined standard contribution limit across traditional and Roth IRAs is $7,500 for eligible individuals younger than 50, or $8,600 for those age 50 or older, subject to taxable compensation and other rules. The 2026 standard employee elective deferral limit for most 401(k), 403(b) and certain governmental 457 plans is $24,500. Eligible plan participants age 50 or older generally have an $8,000 additional catch-up under the usual provisions, with special higher rules for ages 60 to 63 and other conditions. Plan documents can impose additional restrictions.","2026 Roth IRA direct-contribution eligibility phases out at modified adjusted gross income between $153,000 and $168,000 for eligible single/head-of-household taxpayers and $242,000 to $252,000 for eligible joint filers, according to IRS publication guidance. Traditional IRA deductibility uses different income ranges and employer-plan coverage facts. Married filing separately and other statuses require their own rules. A tax-advantaged account's balance is not proof the saver qualifies for every new contribution.","Check employer match separately. A plan may promise, for illustration, a 100% employer match on the first 3% of eligible pay deferred, subject to plan terms and vesting. If eligible pay is $50,000, 3% employee deferral is $1,500 and the employer could contribute another $1,500. This can be valuable, but an employee should not sacrifice essential bills or assume all employer deposits are immediately vested. Compare fees, investment choices and plan restrictions.","Students should not import 2025 dollar limits into a 2026 example, nor assume an IRA and employer-plan deferral share the same annual cap. Records include W-2 earnings, employer plan summary, contribution history, IRA statements, modified adjusted income and any corrective distribution documents. The quantitative exercise must identify the tax year and contribution classification explicitly."],"worked":["2026 eligible under-50 IRA standard aggregate annual limit $7,500; at age 50+ $8,600, subject to compensation and eligibility.","2026 typical employee 401(k) elective deferral standard limit $24,500, excluding possible catch-up.","Hypothetical eligible compensation $50,000 and 3% employee plan deferral = $1,500.","Illustrative 100% match on first 3% = $1,500 employer contribution if specific plan rules are satisfied.","IRAs and 401(k)s have different contribution, match, income and withdrawal rules; verify before claiming a tax benefit."],"practice":[["Can a person contribute $7,500 to a traditional IRA and another $7,500 to a Roth IRA as separate standard limits in 2026?","No. The 2026 $7,500 standard limit is generally an aggregate across traditional and Roth IRA contributions for the individual, subject to compensation and applicable age-based catch-up."],["Why might a 3% employer match not be identical to $1,500 of immediately withdrawable cash?","The example requires qualifying compensation and plan contributions, and employer matching deposits may have vesting, investment, tax and withdrawal restrictions."]]},{"title":"Capital gains, qualified dividends and the tax-lot ledger","body":["A taxable investor must track purchase date, adjusted basis, sale proceeds, distributions, fees and holding period. For a typical stock sale, realized gain or loss is amount realized minus adjusted basis, with special adjustments where relevant. Under federal rules, many capital assets held more than one year are long-term, while assets held one year or less are generally short-term. Federal rate treatment can differ for qualified dividends, ordinary dividends, interest, collectible gains, depreciation-related gains and state income taxes. Calculating net cash received does not automatically calculate taxable gain.","Consider 20 shares purchased at $50 and 20 later purchased at $80, followed by sale of 20 at $100. With proper lot identification of the earlier $50 shares, simplified realized gain is 20×($100−$50)=$1,000. If the later $80 lot is correctly selected and permitted, gain is $400. A $600 difference in realized *gain* is not automatically a $600 reduction in income tax; applicable rates, basis adjustment, wash sales and documentation matter. Broker default lot methods can differ, so confirm instructions before a transaction is executed.","Distributions can generate current taxable income without a share sale. Automatic reinvestment of dividends normally does not eliminate taxable dividend reporting in a taxable brokerage account, and the reinvested amount generally becomes basis in newly purchased shares. A dividend that is reinvested increases the share count, while a corporate price adjustment can offset apparent dividend income; avoid treating dividend yield as free return on top of unchanged price.","Record 1099-DIV, 1099-INT, 1099-B and adjusted basis. The IRS Publication 550 distinguishes capital gain classifications and the treatment of tax losses. State tax and investment-income taxes may apply based on filing status, domicile and income. Tax-aware investing seeks a supportable after-tax outcome rather than simply avoiding all realized gains regardless of the investment's economic risk."],"worked":["Buy 20 shares at $50 ($1,000 basis) and another 20 at $80 ($1,600 basis).","Sell 20 shares at $100 ($2,000 proceeds), ignoring fees.","Identified first lot realized gain $1,000; identified later lot realized gain $400.","Difference in gain $600; actual tax difference depends on applicable legal rate and tax classifications.","A $200 dividend automatically reinvested remains potentially reportable as dividend income in a taxable account and may add $200 basis to new shares."],"practice":[["Why is a $600 tax-lot gain difference not itself a $600 tax saving?","Tax liability applies the appropriate rates and other rules to taxable amounts. A difference in gain changes the tax base but is not a dollar-for-dollar reduction in tax."],["What happens to tax-basis records when a taxable-account dividend is automatically reinvested?","The reinvestment normally purchases new shares with their own cost basis and holding period; the dividend itself can still be reportable as income."]]},{"title":"Loss harvesting, wash sales and the danger of tax-driven overtrading","body":["Tax-loss harvesting realizes a capital loss in a taxable account that may offset capital gains and, under applicable federal rules, a limited amount of ordinary income with additional carryforward treatment. It does not make an economically poor investment harmless. Selling securities at a loss and buying substantially identical securities within the relevant wash-sale window can disallow a current loss deduction and generally adjust basis in replacement securities in certain settings. The rules can reach purchases across accounts or by related parties in circumstances the learner must verify.","Under the common wash-sale rule, compare purchases of substantially identical stock or securities during the period beginning 30 days before and ending 30 days after the loss sale, including the sale date. It is not a rule that simply requires waiting 30 days from the date of buying. Special circumstances, including IRA replacements, can have different permanent or deferred effects and require tax expertise. A fund with a different ticker is not automatically nonidentical.","Suppose a taxable investor buys a security for $5,000, sells for $4,000 and realizes a $1,000 economic loss before fees. The person then buys substantially identical exposure ten days later. Under the assumed wash-sale facts, the immediate loss deduction is disallowed; in many taxable replacement scenarios the disallowed amount adds to replacement basis, deferring tax recognition rather than eliminating the economic loss. If the individual instead changes economic investment risk drastically just to harvest a tax loss, subsequent market moves can outweigh any tax advantage.","IRS Publication 550 describes federal annual capital-loss deduction limitations: a net capital loss may reduce ordinary taxable income by up to $3,000 annually for most individuals or $1,500 for married filing separately, subject to the instructions and carryover. This is not a promise of a refund equal to $3,000. A net loss, filing facts, and available taxable income matter. Build a ledger of sale date, security, cost basis, realized loss, repurchase dates, replacement basis and next-year carryover."],"worked":["Original basis $5,000; sale proceeds $4,000 ⇒ $1,000 realized economic loss before fees.","Substantially identical repurchase 10 days later triggers a federal wash-sale issue in the assumed facts.","In a common taxable replacement, current deduction may be disallowed and replacement basis adjusted by the disallowed loss.","Annual $3,000 net capital-loss limit for offset against ordinary income is conditional on filing status and income.","A $3,000 deduction at 22% illustrative marginal rate saves up to $660 in that simplified scenario, not $3,000 in tax."],"practice":[["Why is a different ETF ticker not conclusive evidence that a substitute purchase avoids the wash-sale rule?","The legal question concerns substantially identical stock or securities and facts of the positions, not just two symbols. Evidence, index methodology and tax guidance matter."],["What is the maximum simplified tax reduction from a permitted $1,000 deduction at a flat illustrative 22% marginal rate?","$220 in this simplified one-rate example, if the deduction is actually available and offsets income at that rate; the original $1,000 investment loss remains real."]]},{"title":"Housing withdrawals, Roth ordering and early-distribution traps","body":["Financing a home purchase from retirement accounts can destroy long-run compounding and create unplanned tax consequences. Under IRS Publication 590-B, certain eligible first-home distributions can qualify for an exception from the 10% additional early-distribution tax, subject to a $10,000 lifetime limit and other requirements. An exception to the additional tax does not automatically make the distribution exempt from ordinary income tax. Retirement accounts and employer plans have different available exceptions, and the relevant first-home definition, timing and eligible family uses must be verified.","Roth IRAs distinguish regular contributions, conversion/rollover amounts and earnings through distribution ordering rules. Regular Roth contributions may generally be withdrawn without income tax or penalty under applicable rules, but this can still undermine long-term saving. Qualified Roth earnings distributions require the relevant five-tax-year holding period and a qualifying condition, which can include certain first-home situations with the $10,000 lifetime restriction. A casual statement that 'all Roth money can be used tax-free for a first house' is false.","Consider a hypothetical traditional IRA holding $20,000, fully attributable to deductible contributions and growth. A 42-year-old withdraws $10,000 for a transaction meeting an eligible first-home exception. The 10% additional tax may be avoided if requirements are satisfied, but the $10,000 distribution can still be included in ordinary income. At an illustrative 22% relevant income-tax rate, that might imply $2,200 tax before other complexities, leaving $7,800 net economic cash from that $10,000 gross withdrawal if not covered by other available funds.","Before recommending such withdrawal, compare delaying the purchase, cash accumulation, legitimate state or lender assistance, and the opportunity cost of removing money from tax-advantaged growth. A taxable-account withdrawal can also trigger capital gains. The proper decision is a dated net-of-tax sources-and-uses worksheet that separates legal eligibility from cash feasibility and long-term consequences."],"worked":["Hypothetical eligible first-home traditional IRA gross distribution $10,000 from a fully taxable account.","Assume valid additional-tax exception; no 10% extra tax under this illustration.","Illustrative 22% income-tax impact $2,200, so after-tax economic cash $7,800.","Lost hypothetical 20-year growth at 6% on withdrawn $10,000 is substantial, but not guaranteed.","Verify Roth contribution ordering and five-tax-year rule separately; cannot universally declare every Roth dollar free of tax."],"practice":[["What is the difference between avoiding a 10% additional tax and avoiding ordinary income tax on a traditional IRA withdrawal?","The 10% penalty exception is a separate rule from inclusion of a taxable retirement distribution in ordinary income. An eligible first-home use may affect the former without eliminating the latter."],["Why might a homeowner still choose not to withdraw Roth contributions even when the withdrawal is legally tax free?","The money loses future tax-advantaged compounding capacity, may not be replaceable above annual contribution limits, and could diminish retirement security or reserves."]]},{"title":"Employer match, savings priority and after-tax model comparisons","body":["Household savings allocation is a constrained optimization problem. A near-term rent, loan payment and emergency cash need creates nonnegotiable dates; an employer match offers a conditional return-like enhancement; high-interest debt creates a contractual repayment obligation; and tax-advantaged retirement saving may improve long-term after-tax wealth. A universal investment hierarchy ignores individual debt rates, plan quality, vesting, taxes and access needs. The first step is to ensure solvency and realistic reserve funding.","Suppose a worker earns $50,000 and an employer contributes an additional dollar per dollar on the first 3% of pay deferred into an eligible plan. A $1,500 employee deferral, if eligible, may receive $1,500 match, creating $3,000 invested before taxes and vesting. Yet if the worker has no way to pay an imminent $1,200 car repair, maxing contributions by borrowing at very high credit-card rates could be financially destructive. A good worksheet compares the marginal benefit of the match against lost liquidity and debt costs.","Now compare a 20-year fixed monthly $250 contribution at a hypothetical nominal 6% before fees and tax. Future value of an ordinary monthly annuity is C×[(1+r)^n−1]/r, with r=0.06/12 and n=240, approximately $115,510. At 4% it would be about $91,694. These are not expected market outcomes. A taxable account with distributions and capital-gains taxes will differ from a qualified Roth account, but exact future tax burden cannot be inferred without basis, cash-flow composition and tax rates.","An investment policy should name the marginal dollar's destination and justify it: reserve, down payment, employer plan, IRA or taxable brokerage. Track whether the projected cash contribution is truly available after recurring bills. Limit and eligibility checks precede tax benefits; investment risk and liquidity constraints remain even when an account is called tax advantaged."],"worked":["Eligible pay $50,000, match 100% of first 3% deferred ⇒ $1,500 worker and $1,500 potential employer contribution.","If worker contributes $250 per month, yearly personal contribution $3,000.","Ordinary annuity 20 years at 6% nominal compounded monthly: about $115,510 before fees and taxes.","At 4%, modeled accumulation is about $91,694.","Any match is conditional on actual plan documents, eligibility, contribution, employer credit and vesting."],"practice":[["Why should a person examine high-interest debt and emergency liquidity before directing all excess cash to long-horizon investing?","Contractual debt expenses and near-term cash obligations can force borrowing or investment liquidation during losses. A balance-sheet and cash-flow review should precede risk taking."],["What input difference drives the $115,510 and $91,694 twenty-year results when monthly contributions are identical?","The assumed nominal annual returns, 6% and 4%, drive different monthly compounding rates. Neither is a guarantee or sufficient basis for ignoring risk."]]},{"title":"Advanced after-tax allocation and inheritance-aware recordkeeping","body":["Long-horizon account selection also affects beneficiaries, estate administration and future tax flexibility. A taxable brokerage asset might have an adjusted basis that changes under qualifying death-time rules; retirement accounts typically have distinct beneficiary and income-tax distribution requirements. A Roth IRA may offer different tax treatment to qualified owner and beneficiary distributions, but inherited account timing rules still matter. A trust named as a beneficiary can change distribution administration and sometimes tax outcomes. This is why Module 3 legal coordination and Module 4 investing should not be taught in isolation.","Tax-aware portfolio management tracks location and tax character of assets. Interest from taxable bonds is generally different from qualified dividend income; funds can distribute taxable capital gains or dividends even when the investor never personally sells shares. Tax-efficient products may reduce distributions but can create tracking, concentration or liquidity tradeoffs. Federal, Connecticut and other state treatment can differ. It is inappropriate to claim any stock fund universally belongs in a Roth IRA solely because its expected return is high.","Build a four-account inventory: taxable brokerage, pretax employer retirement, Roth IRA and a near-term bank deposit. For each record ownership, investment risk, beneficiary designation, basis, contribution limits, withdrawal access and tax documents. If a family plans to give a child property or place the home in a trust, confirm the intended assets' transfer rules independently. Legal estate and income-tax treatment are not the same thing.","Conclude with a formal memo that evaluates net-of-tax rather than merely pre-tax wealth, and states what would change under a different future tax rate or investment result. This is a financial education exercise, not preparation of a real tax return. Source each time-sensitive 2026 threshold from the IRS and avoid claiming that an assumed marginal tax rate equals the taxpayer's actual effective rate."],"worked":["Illustrative taxable stock lot current value $30,000, adjusted basis $18,000 ⇒ $12,000 unrealized gain.","Under a future sale for $30,000, simplified realized gain $12,000 before applicable expenses, losses and tax classification.","If a qualifying inheritance scenario independently supports $30,000 adjusted basis, immediate $30,000 sale could yield near-zero modeled gain subject to exceptions.","An IRA inherited balance may carry different federal income-tax treatment and distribution rules; do not equate it with a taxable stock-lot basis step-up.","A family investment register should identify date, cost basis, asset type, account owner, legal beneficiary and source."],"practice":[["Why can a trust's name in a beneficiary box require specific retirement tax analysis?","Inherited retirement timing and beneficiary classification rules may depend on trust provisions, designated beneficiaries and statutory requirements; legal coordination is necessary."],["What is the difference between an unrealized gain and taxable gain on a future sale?","Unrealized appreciation is an economic change before disposition. A later realized gain generally uses amount realized less adjusted basis under applicable recognition and special rules."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["IRS · IRA 2026 contribution limits","https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/retirement-topics-ira-contribution-limits"],["IRS · 2026 retirement plan and IRA COLA table","https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/cola-increases-for-dollar-limitations-on-benefits-and-contributions"],["IRS · Publication 590-A (2026 updates and Roth income phases)","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p590a"],["IRS · Publication 590-B (withdrawals and first-home exception)","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p590b"],["IRS · Publication 550 (capital gains, tax lots, wash sale)","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p550"],["IRS · Publication 551 (basis and inherited assets)","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p551"],["IRS · 2026 retirement savings release","https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/401k-limit-increases-to-24500-for-2026-ira-limit-increases-to-7500"]],"assessment":{"title":"2026 tax-aware investment and home-financing memorandum","prompt":"Compare taxable brokerage, traditional and Roth IRA, and eligible employer 401(k) strategies for a hypothetical $250/month saver. Check published 2026 limits and eligibility caveats; compute equal-pre-tax-cost traditional versus Roth outcomes under 10%, 20% and 30% future tax rates; select tax lots; analyze a wash sale and first-home IRA withdrawal scenario. Produce a liquidity-preserving Connecticut home savings recommendation and write a separate beneficiary/basis note, with IRS sources.","rubric":["Correct account, owner and income-tax classification (20%)","2026 limits and contribution eligibility (20%)","Tax lot, wash sale and basis calculations (20%)","Home withdrawal and liquidity risk (20%)","After-tax scenario model and source-cited recommendations (20%)"]}}],"capstone":{"title":"Connecticut Homebuyer and Long-Horizon Investment Committee","intro":"You are acting as a financial analyst for a fictional Connecticut household, not recommending securities to a real person. Deliver a valuation comparison, investment policy, after-tax savings schedule and written risk recommendation. All yields, prices and tax rates below are illustrative unless explicitly dated IRS facts.","scenario":["Household gross earnings are $50,000 per year; net monthly household income is $3,900. Essential and recurring obligations are $3,100 per month. The household believes $250–$500 monthly savings is feasible, subject to cash flow and other costs.","Bank cash is $18,000, of which $2,000 is reserved for imminent bills. An emergency cash target of $10,000 must remain intact. A prospective Connecticut closing within 18 months requires another $12,000 of available cash. There is no guaranteed assistance or bonus.","A separate taxable brokerage holds a hypothetical $5,000 stock position; its 50 shares were bought at $100 each. A year later the position might sell at $108 per share and distribute $3 per share. A hypothetical ETF charges 0.10% yearly versus another at 0.80%. No realized or forecast return is guaranteed.","Long-term retirement portfolio proposal has 60% stocks and 40% bonds, with modeling volatilities 20% and 6%, and correlation 0.20. Annual realized stock/bond example returns are +12% and −3%. These risk statistics are supplied model assumptions, not market forecasts.","Employer plan hypothetically matches 100% of the first 3% of $50,000 eligible salary contributed, subject to plan rules and vesting. Federal 2026 IRA and 401(k) statutory limit information is available from the IRS. The household is under age 50 for this case.","Two investment security models: a perpetual dividend stock with next dividend $2, discount rate 9% and growth 3%; a three-year $1,000-face zero-coupon bond with yields 4% and 5%.","For a tax comparison, the household can sacrifice $1,000 of current gross earnings; current marginal tax rate is hypothetically 20%, a future traditional distribution may be taxed at 10%, 20% or 30%, and both retirement vehicles share hypothetical wealth growth factor 2. Roth distributions are assumed qualified solely for the comparison.","A home withdrawal proposal suggests using $10,000 of otherwise fully taxable traditional IRA assets for eligible first-home expenses. Analyze any applicable exception to the 10% additional tax separately from ordinary income tax, and the economic retirement opportunity cost."],"tasks":["Produce a dated uses-and-sources cash table for near-term bills, emergency reserve, home closing and investable funds. Identify the shortfall without inventing assistance.","Compute stock investment total return before fees and taxes; explain what share ownership represents and why an SEC-registered fund is not the same as a bank deposit.","Value the constant-growth dividend model under 9% versus 10% required return, and price the zero-coupon bond at 4% and 5% yields; critique the assumptions.","Calculate 60/40 one-year realized return, ending weights and exact dollar rebalancing. Calculate portfolio annual volatility at correlation 0.20 and correlation 1.0 and explain model uncertainty.","Model 20 years of $250 monthly contributions under 6% and 4% nominal rates; separate contributions and modeled gains and describe real purchasing power risks.","Compare traditional and Roth on equal current *gross-compensation* cost, calculating after-tax terminal value under 10%, 20% and 30% future rates.","Document current-year retirement eligibility, the illustrative employer match, taxable securities basis, income classifications and wash-sale warnings.","Assess the first-home IRA distribution scenario, highlighting the distinction between a permitted additional-tax exception and ordinary income-tax treatment.","Provide a concise investment-policy memo that recommends an evidence-based allocation for the home horizon and a separate long-term portfolio, with explicit triggers for reassessment."],"answers":["Cash after near bills and $10,000 emergency floor is $18,000−$2,000−$10,000=$6,000. A $12,000 closing goal has a $6,000 gap. At $250 per month and no interest this takes 24 deposits, exceeding 18 months; $333.34 per month would cover $6,000 in 18 deposits if feasible.","Stock gross buy cost $5,000; sale value $5,400; dividends $150; total economic gain $550 or 11% before fees and taxes.","Dividend value = $2/(0.09−0.03)=$33.33; at 10% required return = $2/(0.10−0.03)=$28.57. Zero-bond prices $1,000/(1.04³)≈$889.00 and $1,000/(1.05³)≈$863.84.","A 60/40 portfolio with +12% stock and −3% bond annual returns earns 6%: $10,000 becomes $10,600, with stock $6,720 (63.40%). Exact 60/40 rebalance sells $360 stock and buys $360 bond. Variance at ρ=0.2 is 0.016128 and SD about 12.70%; at ρ=1, SD=14.4%.","At $250 monthly over 240 months, contributions total $60,000. Illustrative 6% nominal annual, monthly compounded, gives $115,510.22, versus $91,693.66 at 4%, before fees and inflation.","Equal gross $1,000 earnings: traditional $1,000 invested and Roth $800 after illustrative 20% current tax. After factor-2 growth traditional gross $2,000 pays $1,800 at 10% future tax, $1,600 at 20%, $1,400 at 30%; qualified Roth distributes $1,600 under assumed rules.","2026 standard aggregate under-50 IRA limit $7,500 and standard 401(k) employee deferral $24,500, subject to separate legal requirements. Employer illustrative first-3% match on $50,000 salary yields $1,500 additional plan contribution if all plan conditions are satisfied.","A qualifying first-home IRA use may avoid the 10% additional early-distribution tax within limits; it does not automatically waive ordinary income taxation. At a hypothetical flat relevant 22% income tax on an otherwise fully taxable $10,000 distribution, economic after-tax funds could be $7,800, subject to actual return rules.","A defensible decision protects the home cash need and emergency reserves and does not rely on stock gains by the closing date. Any long-term investing recommendation must reflect the legitimate budget, employer plan, debt, tax status and risk capacity."],"rubric":["Financial integrity and housing liquidity (20%)","Security return, valuation and bond mathematics (20%)","Portfolio variance, drawdown and investment policy (25%)","2026 tax, IRA and employer-plan literacy (20%)","Counterevidence, source dates and written judgment (15%)"],"references":[["SEC Diversification","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/getting-started/asset-allocation"],["SEC Fund Fees 2025","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/general-resources/news-alerts/alerts-bulletins/investor-bulletins/mutual-fund-and-etf-fees-and-expenses-investor-bulletin"],["IRS 2026 Limits","https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/cola-increases-for-dollar-limitations-on-benefits-and-contributions"],["IRS Publication 590-B","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p590b"],["IRS Publication 550","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p550"]]},"edition":"3-academic-addon","restrictions":{"no_actual_investment_predictions":true,"no_persistent_model_inputs":true,"preserve_original_learning":true,"not_accredited":true}};
